<script>
const D={
  tlawrence:{bar:`pos-bar-qb`,pos:`QB`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Trevor Lawrence`,team:`Jacksonville Jaguars · HC: Liam Coen (Yr 2) · OC: Grant Udinski (Yr 2) · Week 2 vs DEN`,stats:[{v:`22.15`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$5,800`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.82x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$5,800</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Trevor Lawrence grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> (Cash) in Week 2, projected for <strong>22.15 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$5,800</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.82, the best of the four quarterbacks on this list. Unlike Young and Williams, this isn't a one-week story: Lawrence's Week 1 (26.1 points) and matchup-only read (16.96 against Denver) both point in a supportive direction, and the blend lands as a genuinely repeatable, safe number rather than a bet on variance holding up.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>QB</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Jacksonville Jaguars</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>DEN</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$5,800</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>22.15</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.82</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>15.50</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>31.01</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>44.5 / JAX +2.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line: 222.2 passing yards, 1.22 passing TDs, 16.4 rushing yards, 0.37 rushing TDs — already a healthy floor before blending in Week 1's 26.1-point actual result.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Consistency across both inputs:</strong> Week 1 (26.1) and the matchup-only read (16.96) don't disagree wildly, which is exactly what supports a Cash-safe grade rather than a boom-or-bust one.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Real rushing floor:</strong> 16.4 rushing yards and 0.37 rushing TDs give Lawrence a separate scoring path beyond his arm.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Denver's own offense has been a Week 1 concern</strong> for opposing defenses generally, which supports a competitive, back-and-forth script here.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Travis Hunter's two-way role and Brian Thomas Jr. at receiver give Lawrence a stable, established set of targets — unchanged from Week 1, which is part of why this projection carries more confidence than the QBs riding a single outlier game.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Liam Coen and OC Grant Udinski both enter Year 2 together, continuing the continuity that fueled last year's 4-13-to-13-4 turnaround. This is the most stable coaching situation of the four QBs evaluated this week.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 15.50 floor at $5,800, backed by agreement between Week 1 actuals and the matchup model, makes this a genuinely strong cash play — exactly the Cash tag this week's build calls for.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">The 31.01 ceiling is respectable, though Lawrence's profile here is built more for consistency than a boom-or-bust spike — a fine complementary tournament piece rather than the centerpiece of a high-leverage build.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Cash).</strong> Grade A, Signal CORE, and the best Value score of this week's quarterbacks — and unlike Young or Williams, both of Lawrence's inputs (Week 1 actual, matchup model) agree, which is exactly what a reliable cash floor needs.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Lawrence is the only quarterback on this list whose Week 1 result (26.1) and matchup-only projection (16.96) both point toward a healthy floor rather than requiring one huge outlier to carry the projection — the definition of a repeatable, cash-safe number.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Lawrence's Week 2 grade doesn't depend on believing in a fluke — both his early-season form and the matchup model agree. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means recognizing that alignment is worth more for cash-game confidence than a bigger, less certain ceiling elsewhere on this list.</p></div>`},
  bijan:{bar:`pos-bar-rb`,pos:`RB`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Bijan Robinson`,team:`Atlanta Falcons · HC: Kevin Stefanski (Yr 1) · OC: Tommy Rees (Yr 1) · Week 2 vs CAR`,stats:[{v:`25.16`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$8,200`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.07x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$8,200</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Bijan Robinson grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> (Cash, Leverage) in Week 2, projected for <strong>25.16 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$8,200</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.07. Both inputs agree here: a strong Week 1 (31.3 points) and a healthy matchup-only read (22.56 against Carolina) point the same direction, giving this the rare combination of a high floor and a price tag that may not have caught up to his expanded role yet.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>RB</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Atlanta Falcons</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>CAR</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$8,200</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>25.16</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.07</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>17.61</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>35.22</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>43.5 / ATL +1.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line against Carolina: 91.6 rushing yards, 0.52 rushing TDs, 4.9 catches, 50.2 receiving yards — already elite volume before folding in Week 1.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Agreement between inputs:</strong> Week 1 (31.3) and the matchup model (22.56) both point toward a strong game, unlike several other backs on this list riding a single outlier week.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Elite dual-threat volume:</strong> nearly 5 catches and over 90 rushing yards projected gives Robinson one of the highest floors of any player on this entire Week 2 slate.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Highest salary on this RB list ($8,200):</strong> the price tag reflects last year's breakout, but the projected role still clears that bar comfortably.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">With Tyler Allgeier gone, Robinson faces no meaningful competition for touches in Atlanta's backfield. New HC Kevin Stefanski and OC Tommy Rees have leaned into exactly the heavy receiving-back usage the early-season numbers reflect.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Stefanski and Rees are in Year 1 together in Atlanta, but the offensive verdict from Week 1 is already in: Robinson's workload matches what this staff built in Cleveland. That's real, validated continuity of approach even without tenure.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 17.61 floor at $8,200 is one of the strongest cash numbers on the entire Week 2 board — exactly the Cash half of this week's tag, with volume that isn't dependent on a specific matchup exploit.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">The Leverage case is about price relative to role: at $8,200, Robinson isn't cheap, but if the field anchors on last year's price memory rather than his fully-realized 2026 workload, there's still real point-for-salary value left on the table given the 35.22 ceiling.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Cash, Leverage).</strong> Grade A, Signal CORE, and agreement between Week 1 actuals and the matchup model — as clean a two-way case as exists on this list.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Robinson's matchup-only Week 2 projection (22.56) and his Week 1 actual (31.3) both clear 22-plus points — the only running back on this list where the “boring” matchup model alone would already justify a strong grade before Week 1 is even factored in.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Robinson doesn't need Week 1 to make his case — the matchup model alone likes him, and the actual result only reinforces it. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means recognizing this as one of the more boring, and therefore more trustworthy, grades on the entire board.</p></div>`},
  shough:{bar:`pos-bar-qb`,pos:`QB`,rec:`Value Play`,recClass:`rec-value`,name:`Tyler Shough`,team:`New Orleans Saints · HC: Kellen Moore (Yr 2) · OC: Doug Nussmeier (Yr 2) · Week 2 vs BAL`,stats:[{v:`18.89`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$5,300`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.56x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`B`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$5,300</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Tyler Shough grades as a <strong>Value Play</strong> (Balanced, Ceiling) in Week 2, projected for <strong>18.89 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$5,300</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.56. His Week 1 (29.2 points) ran well ahead of his prior-year baseline (15.45), and this week's matchup-only read (15.95 against Baltimore) is a step down, landing the blend in between — a genuinely balanced number with real spike upside given his early rushing usage.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>QB</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>New Orleans Saints</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>BAL</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$5,300</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>18.89</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.56</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>13.22</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>26.44</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>VALUE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>47.5 / NO +8.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line against Baltimore: 249.4 passing yards, 0.82 passing TDs, 15.4 rushing yards, 0.31 rushing TDs — real passing volume even as a big underdog.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Significant underdog role (+8.5):</strong> as a large road underdog in a 47.5-point game, New Orleans likely throws often and plays from behind, which supports raw volume even if efficiency suffers.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Real rushing equity:</strong> 15.4 rushing yards and 0.31 rushing TDs give Shough a scoring path beyond the pass, part of why the Ceiling tag applies here.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Baltimore's own defense</strong> enters Week 2 without the continuity of prior years under an entirely new coaching staff, adding some uncertainty to how well they defend early in the season.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Chris Olave remains the clear focal point of this passing game, and Shough's rapport with him carried over from Week 1. Travis Etienne Jr.'s receiving role gives Shough a safety-valve option that supports garbage-time volume in a likely negative game script.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Kellen Moore and OC Doug Nussmeier both enter Year 2 together, giving Shough a full offseason under the same system that produced his Week 1 outburst — real continuity, not a new-scheme variable to price in.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 13.22 floor at $5,300 is playable but not a standout — as a road underdog, the floor here depends on garbage-time volume materializing rather than a clean, controlled game plan.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">The 26.44 ceiling, paired with real rushing equity and a pass-heavy underdog role, gives Shough genuine spike potential — the Ceiling half of this week's tag, and a fit for a Chris Olave/Shough correlated stack.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Value Play (Balanced, Ceiling).</strong> Grade B and Signal VALUE reflect a real, evenly-weighted case between last week's breakout and this week's tougher matchup, with rushing equity providing the spike-week case.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">New Orleans is an 8.5-point road underdog this week — the largest spread of any team on this entire Week 2 list — which is exactly the kind of game script that inflates a mobile quarterback's pass volume and rushing opportunity in the second half.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Shough's grade reflects a genuine tension between a strong Week 1 and a tougher matchup this week, resolved roughly down the middle. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means treating this as a real 50/50 read rather than assuming either data point should dominate.</p></div>`},
