role sticking.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Elevated touchdown rate either way:</strong> 0.55 projected TDs even in the more conservative matchup-only read is a real signal of red-zone trust.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Home favorite (-3.5):</strong> a comfortable spread supports a clean, aggressive game plan.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Watson's role in Green Bay's receiver room appears to have expanded significantly after Week 1 — nothing in the box score suggests a one-off usage pattern tied to a specific defensive weakness rather than a real role change.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Matt LaFleur and OC Adam Stenavich provide rare, multi-year continuity — a stable system in which a role change is more likely to be real than scheme noise.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 13.36 floor at $6,200 is a strong cash number, backed by real touchdown equity even under the more conservative of the two inputs — the Cash half of this week's tag.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 26.71 ceiling, with a real Week 1 precedent for a much bigger game, supports the Ceiling side too — completing the Hybrid profile.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Cash, Balanced, Hybrid).</strong> Grade A and Signal CORE despite a much more modest matchup-only read — the touchdown equity embedded in even the conservative number is what keeps this grade high.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Even the matchup-only model's more conservative catch and yardage projection for Watson still carries a 0.55 receiving touchdown rate — higher than several receivers on this list with much larger raw target projections.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Watson's grade survives even when discounted well below his Week 1 outburst, because the touchdown equity shows up in both versions of the data. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means recognizing that's a stronger signal than volume alone would provide.</p></div>`},
