.2) and the matchup model (20.84) both point to a big game, unlike several players on this list riding a single outlier week.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Large underdog role (+8.5):</strong> as the biggest road underdog on this list, New Orleans likely leans heavily on the passing game, funneling volume toward its clear WR1.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Elevated touchdown rate:</strong> 0.63 projected receiving TDs on 7.7 catches is a real red-zone signal, not just a volume story.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Olave remains the unquestioned focal point of New Orleans' passing game, paired with quarterback Tyler Shough. Rookie WR Jordyn Tyson's expected absence to start the season keeps target competition minimal.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Kellen Moore and OC Doug Nussmeier, both in Year 2, directly presided over Olave's Week 1 breakout — real continuity, not a new-scheme variable.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 14.62 floor at $7,200, backed by agreement between both inputs, makes this one of the safer cash plays at receiver this week — the Balanced half of the tag.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 29.23 ceiling with real touchdown equity in a big-underdog passing game supports the Ceiling half too. A natural pairing with Tyler Shough in tournament stacks.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Balanced, Ceiling).</strong> Grade A, Signal CORE, and agreement between Week 1 actuals and the matchup model — a genuinely complete profile.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Olave's matchup-only Week 2 projection (20.84) and his Week 1 actual (31.2) both clear 20-plus points — a rare case on this list where the “boring” matchup model alone would already justify a strong grade.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Olave doesn't need Week 1 to make his case — the matchup model alone likes him as a clear WR1 in a pass-heavy underdog offense. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means treating this alignment as a real positive, not just a coincidence.</p></div>`},
  bryceyoung:{bar:`pos-bar-qb`,pos:`QB`,rec:`Value Play`,recClass:`rec-value`,name:`Bryce Young`,team:`Carolina Panthers · HC: Dave Canales (Yr 3) · Week 2 vs ATL`,stats:[{v:`19.97`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$5,400`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.70x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`B`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$5,400</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Bryce Young grades as a <strong>Value Play</strong> (Leverage) in Week 2, projected for <strong>19.97 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$5,400</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.70. The number is almost entirely a Week 1 story: the matchup-only model likes him for just 14.46 points against Atlanta, but he put up a 35.44-point explosion in his Week 1 opener, and the blend between that outburst and his modest prior-year baseline (14.81) is what pulls his final number up. As a Leverage play, the appeal here is exactly that gap — the field may still be pricing him off last year's Bryce Young, not the one who just torched a defense in Week 1.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>QB</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Carolina Panthers</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>ATL</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$5,400</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>19.97</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.70</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>13.98</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>27.95</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>VALUE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>43.5 / CAR -1.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Three numbers matter here: a 14.81 prior-year baseline, a 35.44-point Week 1 outburst, and a matchup-only projection of just 14.46 against Atlanta this week — the blended 19.97 sits well below the Week 1 number and well above the matchup-only read.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>The matchup itself is unremarkable:</strong> the model's pure Week 2 regression (14.46) doesn't see anything special about Atlanta specifically.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Week 1 carries real signal:</strong> a 35-point game isn't noise at the margins — something changed, whether it's play design, personnel usage, or matchup-specific variance.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Home, slight favorite:</strong> Carolina is a 1.5-point favorite in a 43.5-point game, a reasonably competitive, pass-supportive environment.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Jalen Coker and Tetairoa McMillan give Young two legitimate outside targets, and Rico Dowdle in the backfield keeps the offense balanced. None of the receiving corps has changed dramatically since Week 1, which is part of why the Week 1 breakout is worth taking somewhat seriously rather than dismissing as a one-week fluke.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Dave Canales enters his 3rd season as head coach, and Young remains in the same offensive system that produced the Week 1 result. No coaching change to explain away the outburst — whatever clicked in Week 1 is still the same staff and scheme this week.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">As a Leverage-tagged play, this isn't the pitch — a 13.98 floor at $5,400 is playable but unremarkable for cash. The appeal here is squarely the ceiling-to-ownership gap, not a safe floor.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">This is the actual thesis: if the field still prices Young off his career-long modest baseline rather than his Week 1 outburst, he could be a low-owned piece with a real 27.95 ceiling. That's the definition of leverage — point-for-point value the field hasn't caught up to yet.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Value Play (Leverage).</strong> The matchup alone doesn't scream opportunity, but the gap between Young's Week 1 result and his likely Week 2 ownership is the entire case for rostering him as a contrarian piece.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Young's matchup-only projection for Week 2 (14.46) is barely above his own prior-year baseline (14.81) — the entire reason his blended number sits at 19.97 instead is a single 35-point Week 1 outburst that the field may not have fully priced in yet.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Young's Week 2 grade is a direct product of one huge Week 1 game colliding with an unremarkable matchup model. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means being honest that this is a bet on a trend continuing, not a matchup exploit — exactly the profile a Leverage play should be.</p></div>`},
  swift:{bar:`pos-bar-rb`,pos:`RB`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`D'Andre Swift`,team:`Chicago Bears · HC: Ben Johnson (Yr 2) · Week 2 vs MIN`,stats:[{v:`20.09`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$6,300`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.19x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$6,300</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">D'Andre Swift grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> (Cash, Ceiling) in Week 2, projected for <strong>20.09 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$6,300</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.19. A huge Week 1 (35.4 points) landed well above the matchup-only read (12.6 against Minnesota), and the blend still supports a strong grade — a real Cash-and-Ceiling combination if the expanded role from Week 1 is genuinely sticking.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>RB</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Chicago Bears</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>MIN</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$6,300</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>20.09</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.19</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>14.06</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>28.12</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>49.5 / CHI -5.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line against Minnesota: 72.1 rushing yards, 0.45 rushing TDs, modest receiving work — solid but not the story here on its own.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Highest game total on this list (49.5):</strong> paired with Chicago favored by 5.5, this is the most favorable scoring environment of any game evaluated this week.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Real gap between inputs:</strong> the 35.4-point Week 1 output far outpaces the matchup-only read, meaning some of this grade depends on the expanded role continuing.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Home favorite role supports a clean game plan:</strong> a 5.5-point favorite in a shootout-shaped game typically means volume in all situations, not just from behind.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Swift shares this backfield with Caleb Williams in the same high-total Bears offense. Swift's specific role (receiving work plus early-down volume) is what drove the Week 1 outburst, and nothing in the box score suggests that role has been reduced.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Ben Johnson's offense in Chicago is an aggressive, ceiling-raising scheme in his 2nd season that's already shown it can produce big games for multiple skill players at once.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 14.06 floor at $6,300, in the highest-total game on the board, gives Swift a genuinely strong cash number — the Cash half of this week's tag.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 28.12 ceiling in a shootout-shaped game supports the Ceiling half too. Swift is a natural pairing with Caleb Williams in a Bears-stack tournament build.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Cash, Ceiling).</strong> Grade A and Signal CORE, in the best scoring environment of the week. The gap between his Week 1 output and the matchup-only model is real, but the game environment alone supports confidence here.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">This game's 49.5-point total is the highest of any matchup on this Week 2 list — and Swift's Week 1 output (35.4) already showed what his role looks like in exactly this kind of shootout-shaped script.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Swift's grade leans on both a favorable game environment and a real early-season role change. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means watching whether that role holds, since it's doing more work here than the matchup model alone would justify.</p></div>`},
  henry:{bar:`pos-bar-rb`,pos:`RB`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Derrick Henry`,team:`Baltimore Ravens · HC: Jesse Minter (Yr 1) · OC: Declan Doyle (Yr 1) · Week 2 vs NO`,stats:[{v:`23.10`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$7,200`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.21x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$7,200</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Derrick Henry grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> (Balanced, Ceiling, Hybrid) in Week 2, projected for <strong>23.10 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$7,200</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.21. His Week 1 (38.3 points) was one of the biggest individual performances on this entire list, well above the matchup-only read (14.96 against New Orleans) — the definition of a Hybrid case where the blended number still supports a strong grade even discounted well below the outlier.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>RB</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Baltimore Ravens</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>NO</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$7,200</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>23.10</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.21</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>16.17</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>32.34</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>47.5 / BAL -8.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line against New Orleans: 96.7 rushing yards, 0.71 rushing TDs — already strong on its own, before folding in the Week 1 outlier.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Massive favorite (-8.5):</strong> the largest home favorite spread on this list, exactly the game script that supports heavy clock-control rushing volume and goal-line work in the second half.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Elevated touchdown equity either way:</strong> a 0.71 rushing TD projection from the matchup model alone is elite, and Henry's track record of converting short-yardage work into scores is well established.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>New Orleans' own defense</strong> enters as a road underdog by 8.5, a setup that typically leads to a Ravens-favorable game script throughout.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Henry remains the unquestioned workhorse in Baltimore's backfield with minimal receiving competition, and the passing game around him (Lamar Jackson, Zay Flowers) keeps defenses from fully committing to stopping the run.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Baltimore's new coaching staff (HC Jesse Minter, OC Declan Doyle, both Year 1) is a major continuity change, but Henry's individual role — lead back, goal-line work — is scheme-agnostic enough that it's less exposed to playcalling uncertainty.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 16.17 floor at $7,200, backed by a massive favorite spread and elite touchdown equity, is one of the strongest cash numbers on the entire Week 2 board.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 32.34 ceiling, with a real Week 1 precedent for an even bigger outcome, gives Henry legitimate tournament-anchor status too — a rare player who checks every build-type box this week.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Balanced, Ceiling, Hybrid).</strong> Grade A, Signal CORE, and a favorite spread that supports both the floor and ceiling cases simultaneously — as complete a profile as exists on this list.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Baltimore is an 8.5-point favorite this week — the largest of any team on this list — and Henry's own matchup-only touchdown projection (0.71 rushing TDs) is elite even before accounting for his 38.3-point Week 1 outburst.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Henry's grade doesn't need Week 1 to hold up — the matchup model and the favorite spread both independently support a strong outlook. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means recognizing this as one of the safest bets on the entire board, ceiling included.</p></div>`},
  jaxdst:{bar:`pos-bar-dst`,pos:`D/ST`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Jacksonville Jaguars`,team:`Jacksonville Jaguars · HC: Liam Coen (Yr 2) · DC: Anthony Campanile (Yr 2) · Week 2 vs DEN`,stats:[{v:`9.38`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$2,400`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.91x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$2,400</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">The Jacksonville Jaguars D/ST grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> (Cash, Leverage) in Week 2, projected for <strong>9.38 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$2,400</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.91, the best of any player evaluated this week. A strong Week 1 (13 points) reinforces a modest matchup-only read (4.29 against Denver), and at the cheapest salary on this entire list, the case for both cash safety and tournament leverage is compelling.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>D/ST</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Jacksonville Jaguars</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>DEN</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$2,400</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>9.38</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.91</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>5.63</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>14.07</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>44.5 / JAX +2.5</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line against Denver: 1.07 sacks, 1.2 INTs, 0.41 fumble recoveries, 20.3 points allowed — modest on its own, but Jacksonville's Week 1 actual (13 points) suggests real early-season form.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Cheapest salary on the entire Week 2 list ($2,400):</strong> at this price, even a modest floor produces an elite Value score.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Denver's offense</strong> enters this game without any specific standout weakness, but the takeaway volume (1.2 projected INTs) is real regardless.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Real continuity:</strong> DC Anthony Campanile is in his 2nd season, building on last year's dramatic turnaround.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The same core (Josh Hines-Allen, Foye Oluokun, Antonio Johnson) that drove last year's takeaway-heavy defense returns largely intact, with Travis Hunter's expanding role adding further secondary upside.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Liam Coen and DC Anthony Campanile, both in Year 2, provide a stable program building on a historic turnaround.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 5.63 floor at just $2,400 is an excellent cash number purely on a price basis — minimal roster cost for a real defensive floor.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 14.07 ceiling at this salary is exceptional tournament value — the combination of a rock-bottom price and a real Week 1 track record is exactly what a Leverage-tagged D/ST should look like.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Cash, Leverage).</strong> Grade A, Signal CORE, and the best Value score of any player evaluated this week — an easy, low-cost roster inclusion across build types.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">At $2,400, the Jaguars carry the single highest Value score (3.91) of any of the 28 players evaluated this week — a direct function of a rock-bottom salary and a real Week 1 track record.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: this is about as close to a free roll as exists on the Week 2 board — minimal salary commitment for a defense with real recent form. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means not overthinking a genuinely clean, cheap value play.</p></div>`},
  goedert:{bar:`pos-bar-te`,pos:`TE`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Dallas Goedert`,team:`Philadelphia Eagles · HC: Nick Sirianni (Yr 6) · OC: Sean Mannion (Yr 1) · Week 2 vs TEN`,stats:[{v:`15.39`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$4,800`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`3.21x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$4,800</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Dallas Goedert grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> (Cash) in Week 2, projected for <strong>15.39 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$4,800</strong> salary — a Value score of 3.21. A strong Week 1 (23.7 points) sits above the matchup-only read (14.63 against Tennessee), and the blend still supports a strong grade — real touchdown equity (1.13 projected TDs) at a cheap-for-his-production salary.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>TE</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Philadelphia Eagles</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>TEN</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$4,800</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>15.39</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>3.21</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>10.77</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>21.55</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>39.5 / PHI -7</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The matchup-only line against Tennessee: 3.6 catches, 42.5 receiving yards, and a striking 1.13 projected receiving TDs — elite red-zone equity independent of Week 1.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Home favorite (-7):</strong> a comfortable spread supports a game plan that can lean on short-yardage and red-zone concepts, exactly where Goedert's value concentrates.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Elite touchdown rate:</strong> north of one projected touchdown on modest catch volume is one of the highest TD-per-target rates of any player on this entire Week 2 list.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Low total (39.5):</strong> the one check on ceiling — fewer overall plays could cap the raw yardage upside even with strong TD equity.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Goedert remains Philadelphia's clear No. 1 tight end target with A.J. Brown now in New England and the receiving corps otherwise reshuffled around DeVonta Smith. No internal competition threatens his role.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Nick Sirianni (6th season) and new OC Sean Mannion are now two games into their first season together — Goedert's Week 1 role held up as expected, a real positive early signal for this new offense's tight end usage.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 10.77 floor at $4,800, backed by elite touchdown equity as a home favorite, makes this one of the better cash tight end plays this week.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 21.55 ceiling exists, though the low game total caps some upside relative to tight ends in higher-scoring games this week.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Cash).</strong> Grade A, Signal CORE, and elite red-zone equity at a reasonable salary — a strong floor play at the position.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Goedert's matchup-only projection includes 1.13 receiving touchdowns on just 3.6 catches — one of the highest touchdown-per-target rates of any player, at any position, on this entire Week 2 list.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Goedert's case here is built on real touchdown equity as a comfortable home favorite, not a low-total ceiling chase. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means recognizing that's a genuinely strong cash floor even with a modest raw yardage outlook.</p></div>`},
