><strong>Road underdog (+5.5):</strong> paired with the high total, this points toward a competitive, offense-heavy game rather than a defensive struggle.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Cheap salary ($2,600)</strong> helps offset the modest raw production expectation.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Minnesota's defensive personnel is unchanged from Week 1, and the unit's own offense (Justin Jefferson, T.J. Hockenson) plays into the same high-scoring game environment working against this defense's raw upside.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC Kevin O'Connell's program provides real continuity, though the defensive circumstances this week (highest total on the board, playing a red-hot Bears offense under new HC Ben Johnson) are genuinely difficult.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 4.80 floor at $2,600 is fair for the price, though the shootout-shaped game environment caps expectations for a big defensive scoring day.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 12.00 ceiling exists but isn't a standout given the difficult scoring environment — a fine cheap piece rather than a centerpiece tournament defense.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play (Balanced), with a real game-environment caveat.</strong> Grade A and Signal CORE reflect a fair price-to-production match, but the highest game total on the board is a genuine headwind for defensive scoring specifically.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">This game carries the highest total of any matchup on this entire Week 2 list (49.5) — a genuinely difficult environment for a defense to rack up sacks, takeaways, and points-allowed bonuses, whatever the underlying talent level.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: Minnesota's CORE grade reflects a fair price for a modest expectation, not a hidden exploit. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means weighing the shootout-shaped game environment honestly against the raw salary efficiency.</p></div>`},
  hutchinson:{bar:`pos-bar-wr`,pos:`WR`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Xavier Hutchinson`,team:`Houston Texans · HC: DeMeco Ryans (Yr 4) · OC: Nick Caley (Yr 1) · Week 2 vs CIN`,stats:[{v:`17.22`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$3,500`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`4.92x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$3,500</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Xavier Hutchinson grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> in Week 2, projected for <strong>17.22 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$3,500</strong> salary — a Value score of 4.92. Like Kayshon Boutte on the same team, this projection is driven almost entirely by Nico Collins being ruled OUT rather than any established role of his own — Hutchinson hasn't recorded a game yet this season.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>WR</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Houston Texans</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>CIN</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$3,500</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>17.22</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>4.92</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>12.05</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>24.10</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>46.5 / HOU -3</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">The underlying inputs here are even thinner than Boutte's.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Zero games recorded this season:</strong> unlike most players in this series, there's no actual 2026 snapshot to validate against — this projection is built entirely on a prior-year baseline (5.65) plus a manual adjustment for opportunity.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Behind Boutte in the target hierarchy:</strong> the lower salary and smaller blended-projection bump relative to Boutte suggest the model sees him as the secondary, not primary, beneficiary of Collins' absence.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Home favorite (-3)</strong> in a 46.5-point game supports real offensive volume for Houston as a whole.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Hutchinson shares this target vacuum with Kayshon Boutte. Nico Collins' OUT status is the entire structural reason either player has meaningful salary this week.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">DeMeco Ryans (4th season) and OC Nick Caley (1st season) haven't had to distribute targets without Collins on the field yet this season — real uncertainty in exactly how this specific target vacuum gets split.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 12.05 floor at $3,500 is speculative given zero recorded 2026 snaps — treat this as a real cash consideration only with same-day confirmation of snap counts and target share, not a pre-lock lock.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 24.10 ceiling at just $3,500 offers real salary relief for a tournament build if the target distribution favors him, though Boutte appears to be the model's preferred beneficiary between the two.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play, with the same data-integrity caveat as Kayshon Boutte.</strong> This is a legitimate opportunity thesis, not an established role — treat the specific numbers as speculative until snap counts confirm the target split.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Hutchinson has zero recorded games this season, yet carries a 17.22-point blended projection built almost entirely on Nico Collins' absence — a real reminder that this number reflects opportunity, not any track record of his own.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: this is the more speculative of the two Houston target-vacuum plays this week. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means treating Hutchinson as a real, but secondary, beneficiary behind Boutte until the target split is confirmed.</p></div>`},
  boutte:{bar:`pos-bar-wr`,pos:`WR`,rec:`Core Play`,recClass:`rec-core`,name:`Kayshon Boutte`,team:`Houston Texans · HC: DeMeco Ryans (Yr 4) · OC: Nick Caley (Yr 1) · Week 2 vs CIN`,stats:[{v:`28.55`,k:`Proj. DK Pts`},{v:`$4,000`,k:`DK Salary`},{v:`7.14x`,k:`Value Score`},{v:`A`,k:`Grade`}],salary:`DK Week 2 Salary: <span>$4,000</span>`,body:`<div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Summary</div><p class="ms-body">Kayshon Boutte grades as a <strong>Core Play</strong> in Week 2, projected for <strong>28.55 DK points</strong> at a <strong>$4,000</strong> salary — the single highest Value score (7.14) of any player evaluated across this entire Week 2 series. This number does not come from Boutte's own recent form or a strong matchup read — it's a direct, manual reflection of Nico Collins being ruled OUT, opening up Houston's WR1 role. Treat this as a usage-shift projection, not an organic one.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Player Snapshot</div><table class="modal-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Position</td><td>WR</td></tr><tr><td>Team</td><td>Houston Texans</td></tr><tr><td>Opponent</td><td>CIN</td></tr><tr><td>Salary</td><td>$4,000</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>28.55</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>7.14</td></tr><tr><td>Floor</td><td>19.98</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>39.96</td></tr><tr><td>Grade</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Signal</td><td>CORE</td></tr><tr><td>Vegas Total / Spread</td><td>46.5 / HOU -3</td></tr><tr><td>Slate</td><td>Main Slate (Sunday)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Opportunity Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">This is the clearest case in this entire series of a projection driven by opportunity, not track record.</p><div class="str-grid"><div class="str-item"><strong>Every organic input is modest:</strong> Boutte's matchup-only read (6.67), Week 1 actual (3.1), and prior-year baseline (9.08) are all well below his 28.55 blended number — the gap only makes sense as a direct adjustment for Nico Collins' Week 2 absence.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Collins is officially OUT</strong> (salary pulled to $0 on the board), removing Houston's clear WR1 and opening a real target vacuum behind him.</div><div class="str-item"><strong>Home favorite (-3):</strong> a comfortable spread supports a full offensive workload for whoever absorbs Collins' route share.</div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Supporting Cast Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">Boutte and Xavier Hutchinson are the two Houston receivers positioned to absorb Collins' vacated targets. Boutte's higher salary and larger blended-projection bump suggest the model views him as the primary beneficiary, with Hutchinson as the secondary option.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Coaching Scheme and Analysis</div><p class="ms-body">HC DeMeco Ryans (4th season) and OC Nick Caley (1st season, hired from the Rams) are in Week 2 together. Caley's early-season scheme hasn't been tested with a receiver room missing its clear WR1, which adds real uncertainty on top of the opportunity itself.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Cash &amp; GPP Outlook</div><div class="outlook-grid"><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label cash">Cash Game</div><p class="outlook-body">A 19.98 floor at just $4,000 would be exceptional if the role bump is real — but given how speculative the underlying data is, treat this as a real cash consideration only with same-day confirmation of snap counts and target share, not a pre-lock lock.</p></div><div class="outlook-box"><div class="outlook-label gpp">GPP Tournament</div><p class="outlook-body">A 39.96 ceiling at $4,000 is one of the best raw ceiling-to-salary numbers in this entire series — exactly the profile a tournament build wants if the role materializes as projected.</p></div></div></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Slate-IQ Recommendation</div><p class="ms-body"><strong>Core Play, with a real data-integrity caveat.</strong> Grade A and Signal CORE here are the product of a significant manual adjustment for Collins' absence, not organic form. The thesis is sound (WR1 role opening up), but the size of the specific numbers deserves real skepticism until confirmed close to lock.</p></div><div class="modal-callout"><div class="callout-label">Numbers Don't Lie</div><p class="callout-body">Boutte's blended projection (28.55) is more than four times his own matchup-only read (6.67) and more than nine times his Week 1 actual (3.1) — by far the largest gap of any player in this entire series, and clear evidence this number reflects Nico Collins' absence rather than Boutte's own established form.</p></div><div class="modal-section"><div class="ms-heading">Final Thoughts</div><p class="ms-body">Process over emotion: the thesis behind this projection — a WR1 role opening up for a home favorite — is sound. Quality Data Leads to Smart Decisions means being explicit that the specific number (28.55) is a projection of opportunity, not a reflection of anything Boutte has actually done, and it deserves a same-day check before trusting it at face value.</p></div>`}
};

function openModal(id){
  const p=D[id];if(!p)return;
  document.getElementById('m-bar').className='modal-top-bar '+p.bar;
  document.getElementById('m-badges').innerHTML='<span class="pos-badge pos-badge-'+p.bar.replace('pos-bar-','')+'">'+p.pos+'</span><span class="rec-badge '+p.recClass+'">'+p.rec+'</span><button class="modal-close" onclick="closeModal()">Close \u2715</button>';
  document.getElementById('m-name').textContent=p.name;
  document.getElementById('m-team').textContent=p.team;
  document.getElementById('m-stats').innerHTML=p.stats.map(s=>'<div class="modal-stat-cell"><div class="modal-stat-val">'+s.v+'</div><div class="modal-stat-key">'+s.k+'</div></div>').join('');
  document.getElementById('m-body').innerHTML=p.body;
  document.getElementById('m-salary').innerHTML=p.salary;
  document.getElementById('overlay').classList.add('open');
  document.body.style.overflow='hidden';
  document.getElementById('overlay').scrollTop=0;
}
function closeModal(){document.getElementById('overlay').classList.remove('open');document.body.style.overflow=''}
function handleOverlayClick(e){if(e.target===document.getElementById('overlay'))closeModal()}
function filterCards(f,btn){
  document.querySelectorAll('.filter-btn').forEach(b=>b.classList.remove('active'));
  btn.classList.add('active');
  document.querySelectorAll('.eval-card').forEach(c=>{
    const cp=c.dataset.pos;
    const show=(f==='all')||(cp===f);
    c.style.display=show?'':'none';
  });
}
document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(document.getElementById('overlay').classList.contains('open')&&e.key==='Escape')closeModal()});
</script>
</body>
</html>
